  "id": "ex-16-reflexive-verben-b1",
  "intro": "Standalone drill set, not tied to any lesson. The same reflexive verb can take an Akkusativ or a Dativ reflexive pronoun depending on whether there's already an Akkusativ object in the sentence: Ich wasche MICH (no object → Akkusativ) vs. Ich wasche MIR die Hände (die Hände is the Akkusativ object, so the reflexive pronoun shifts to Dativ). The distinction only really shows up for ich/du (mich/mir, dich/dir) — er/sie/es, wir, ihr, and sie/Sie use sich/uns/euch either way, since those forms are identical in both cases. Blocks: A Basistraining → B Vertiefung → C Sprachbausteine → D Wiederholung. Answers in solutions.md.\n",
  "lesson": "EX/16-reflexive-verben-b1",
  "level": "B1",
  "summary": null,
  "title": "Reflexive Verben: Akkusativ- und Dativ-Reflexiv — B1",
  "topic": "Reflexive Verben"
}
